he sufi philosopher ibn sabahin and his  student ashushtari became essentially  absorbed into the shadowy order  important for the development of the  order into dominance in the region are  also later developments and further  branches of the order particularly the  teachings and movement of muhammad  al-jazeera a shadowly master whose  branch is often called the chad lia  jazuliyah or simply jasolia and which  created a form of sufism that was  heavily involved in the politics of the  region and the development of sharifism  the idea that political and religious  authority was primarily the privileged  right of the descendants of the prophet  muhammad which has heavily shaped  morocco to this day  al-jazeera lee wrote the very popular  prayer book called dalai ilharat which  is specifically dedicated to prayers and  supplications for the prophet muhammad  indeed jesus and his followers would  place an enormous emphasis on the role  of the prophet and his role in the  spiritual quest even more so than other  sufi movements which is really saying  something according to jamal abu nasser  the dalai lama made veneration of the  prophet the core of sufi piety the  jesuit movement became so popular that  it solidified the shadowly order as the  most dominant in the maghrib and  reshaped the region's entire spiritual  and religious landscape  the heavy emphasis on venerating the  prophet muhammad would become an  important feature of moroccan sufism in  particular and later referred to as the  tariqa muhammadiyah  if you read sufi poetry from this region  from the last few hundred years there is  an unusually strong focus on praising  the prophets and this also explains the  connection with the political ideas of  sharifism  in general the particular teachings and  practices of the chattalia are difficult  to talk about because just like other  orders like the kadaria the shadolis  have never had a single head of  authority and neither did the early  leaders of the community establish any  particular rules or way of doing things  we can play it safe by saying that the  shadily order practiced the most  universal sufi ritual known as thikr or  remembrance where names of god or the  muslim proclamation of faith is repeated  for long periods of time one will often  have regular gatherings called hadra in  which practices like these are performed  and as with all other orders the  shadolis have their own weird or prayers  and prayer cycles that are given to  initiates by their sheikh and which are  recited either privately and in or in  group  allah in fact did write a treatise  dedicated to vikkit in particular called  miftah falah the key to salvation in  which he defends the practice as well as  its vocal performance in particular so  that is the  vocal chanting of vikkan that is perhaps  the most common in sufism where people  will sit in groups and will they will  chant vocally sometimes pretty loudly  the names of god or the proclamation of  faith some  other muslims and even sufi muslims were  against this idea of doing it vocally  but most weren't and ibna ta'ala writes  this treatise  where he among other things defends this  uh vocal practice of thicker so we know  that the shadows even in the early  periods did perform thicker and that  they did so vocally but other than these  basic practices there is really not much  that characterizes the chadelia in  particular  and because it has spread so widely  across north africa as well as the rest  of the world we see a lot of variety and  diversity between regions and branches  of the order so it's difficult to say  something in particular  that characterizes the order in general  just as in many other sufi traditions  the shadolist view as important the  visiting or ziara to the shrine of  ashadhi where he is buried which is in  humaith egypt as i mentioned and they  will often gather there annually usually  on his birthday or maulid going to this  one village in in egypt and they will do  vikker or vocal chanting together  sometimes sing with percussion  instruments they will pray together and  other  rituals related to the shadowly  practices the shadowly order is often  described as a sober sufi order what  this means is that they usually don't  take part in more ecstatic practices  such as the elaborate sama  rituals or music rituals of other orders  like the mev levia for example with the  very famous whirling dervishes related  to this it is also claimed that the  chavelia is a sufi order that adheres  very strictly to islamic law now i would  be very careful about claims like this  because basically all sufi orders very  strictly follow islamic law at least as  they understand it but this means that  just like all other muslims the  shadolists pray five times a day they do  the hajj to mecca they give to charity  and all the other obligations that other  muslims follow aside from their  supergatory and additional prayers and  practices that they do as part of the  sufi way so to say i think what is  usually meant with this following  islamic law strictly thing is the  previous point about the order being  more sober quote unquote which is a  accurate enough claim i guess i think it  might also have something to do with the  fact that  as i said earlier there isn't a lot that  characterizes the the chandelier in  particular like many other sufj orders  for example if we talk about the mev  levy order like if you ask me to  describe the medley order there are like  clear characteristics like all the  whirling dervishes the elaborate sama  rituals uh if you ask me to describe the  chishtia order always the the kawali  music and these sama practices there  okay if you ask me to to talk about the  defy order well they have the very  elaborate sort of rituals involving  swords and like and when we talk about  the shadowy order it's like  they're muslims like there isn't  anything that stands out in the same way  about the chandelier in comparison to  many other orders around the world i  think that's why some may feel that the  shadowy order is more in in line with  islamic law they seem more like regular  muslims and all this is very problematic  of course but  if i had to guess i think it has  something to do with that too but like i  said because of the lack of any distinct  rules within the order and it's  widespread there is huge diversity  within the shadowly order some branches  are very politically unsocially active  others are more quietest some branches  practice sama or ritualize listening to  music others don't and so on  over the centuries the chavelia has  developed several sub-branches all with  characteristic features and practices of  their own one of the most significant of  these branches has been the shadolia  darakawiya founded by the moroccan  muhammad al-arabi al-darkawi  this branch is sometimes characterized  by a doctrinal adherence to the school  of the andalusian master ibn arabi and  his ideas of the unity of being as can  be seen from the writings of darakawi  masters like ahmed ibn ajiba  but it has also become probably the most  widespread globally and attracted many  westerners in particular  one figure from the darakawi linear that  has become especially formative in this  later regard is the algerian ahmed  al-alawi on which there is an amazing  book called a sufi saint of the 20th  century by the author martin lings  indeed as many westerners started to  become interested in sufism and islam  around the turn of the 20th century it  was often to the shadowly order in  particular that they turned the swedish  painter ivan aguili is probably the  first of these converts he had a special  fascination with the teachings of ibn  arabi and joined the shadily order while  living in egypt  aguili was the person who in turn  initiated the famous rene guenon and  here we see the start of the so-called  traditionalist school or the school of  perennial philosophy  this is a movement that has emerged in  the last century that emphasizes a  perennial wisdom that hides within all  of the world's quote-unquote true  religions often with the view that sufi  oriented islam is the most complete  expression of this perennial wisdom the  representatives of this school are often  followers of ahmed al-alawi in  particular who kind of created a branch  of his own within the shadolia darqawiya  which is often called the chavelia  alawia now these branches and super  branches often get very complicated and  complex but stay with me here in a case  this perennial school often associated  with the shadily order in particular has  been represented by figures like the  already mentioned rene guenon fritschev  shuwon martin lynx and the current most  prominent teacher and philosopher syed  jose nasser  this group of scholars have also been  instrumental in bringing the work of  many sufi scholars of history to a  western audience such as many  translations and interpretations of  figures like ibn arabi and mulasadra and  they do shape our understanding of these  figures to a massive degree but not all  prominent shadily figures in the west  are connected to the traditionalist  school or the alawia the american  islamic scholar no hameem keller is also  an adherent of the shadoli darqawi order  and is often considered one of the most  important muslim teachers in the world  today through his many publications and  commitment to online education we should  also mention the recently passed  american sufi nourodin durki may he rest  in peace who further spread the order to  the u.s and very interestingly also has  connections to other contemporary  figures like ramdas and was actually  largely responsible in his youth for the  publication of his very popular work  called be here now  it is because of the popularity of the  shadolia among scholars and western  converts of high intellectual status  that the shadowy order has become famous  as an order that in particular seems to  attract people of very high education or  intellectual prominence or whatever you  want to call it  it's hard to say why this is or if this  is even accurate to begin with but it  might have something to do with the  so-called sober approach of the order as  well as the flexibility that it has  because it has become so widespread and  that it doesn't have any particularly  fixed rules and so on  that's one guess as to why it has  attracted so many from the west more so  perhaps i would argue that it has  something to do with the historical  circumstances in which this order was  accessible to the figures of the early  20th century so as you can tell the  chavalia is a sufi order that has not  only managed to survive almost 900 years  but has also spread to places all across  the world  the community started by abul hasan  ashavi in the 13th century has become a  decisive factor in the history of north  african sufism through his incorporation  of local maghrib slash and lucian sufi  traditions with the tariqa based sufism  of the east and the later firm  establishment of his authority into an  institutionalized tariqa by successors  like ibn  the shadowy order brought a slightly new  kind of islamic mysticism to the western  lands of the islamic world  ashadhali stands together with abu  madyan as perhaps the most important  figures in the western sufi tradition  and the order that was founded based on  him came to dominate this region for the  rest of history later developments in  the order further shaped the political  and religious landscape of the maghreb  and lately has also come to shape the  religious landscape of europe and north  america so the shadily order is not only  one of the oldest surviving sufi orders  in history it is also absolutely one of  the most important  i'll see you next time  i would like to express my deepest  gratitude to all of my patrons who keep  this channel going through their support  and as always a special shout out to all  of my new saints including nicholas cano  prophet's apprentice  rodenborn and abbas akbar thank you all  so much  [Music]  you
 

